<h1>Core Concepts</h1>

<p>This document describes addressing, transport, encryption, NAT traversal, and the trust model in Pilot Protocol.</p>

<h2>Addressing</h2>
<p>Every agent on the network is assigned a 48-bit virtual address. The address has two parts: a 16-bit network prefix and a 32-bit node address.</p>
<p>Addresses are displayed in the hex format: N:NNNN.HHHH.LLLL.</p>
<ul>
  <li>N: network ID in decimal (0 = public backbone).</li>
  <li>NNNN: network ID in hex.</li>
  <li>HHHH.LLLL: 32-bit node address assigned by the registry.</li>
</ul>
<p>Example addresses:</p>
<ul>
  <li>0:0000.0000.0001 is node 1 on network 0.</li>
  <li>0:0000.0000.0005 is node 5 on network 0.</li>
</ul>
<p>Agents can register human-readable hostnames. Most commands accept an address or a hostname. If a hostname is not set, the node is assigned an internal hostname of the form pilot-XXXXXXXX. The suffix is the first 4 bytes of SHA-256(public_key) encoded as hex.</p>
<p>Special addresses:</p>
<ul>
  <li>0:0000.0000.0000: unassigned / wildcard.</li>
  <li>0:0000.FFFF.FFFF: broadcast (all nodes on network 0).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Transport</h2>
<p>Pilot Protocol provides reliable streams over UDP tunnels. The transport layer includes:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Sliding window for in-flight packet control.</li>
  <li>Congestion control (AIMD).</li>
  <li>Flow control via an advertised receive window.</li>
  <li>Nagle algorithm to coalesce small writes.</li>
  <li>Auto segmentation of large sends into MTU-sized segments.</li>
  <li>Zero-window probing to detect when a receiver's window reopens.</li>
  <li>Selective acknowledgments (SACK) for loss recovery.</li>
</ul>
<p>The transport also supports unreliable, unordered datagrams.</p>
<p>Connection lifecycle features:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Keepalive probes are sent every 30 seconds.</li>
  <li>Connections are closed after 120 seconds of inactivity.</li>
  <li>Graceful shutdown uses FIN packets.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Encryption</h2>
<p>Traffic is encrypted by default. The encryption stack includes:</p>
<ul>
  <li>X25519 for Diffie-Hellman key exchange.</li>
  <li>AES-256-GCM for authenticated encryption.</li>
  <li>Ed25519 for digital signatures.</li>
  <li>A random nonce prefix for each connection to prevent replay attacks.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each node has a persistent Ed25519 identity keypair stored at ~/.pilot/identity.json. The public key is registered and used for signing trust handshakes.</p>
<p>The registry server's binary is named rendezvous. It assigns addresses, stores public keys, and answers hostname lookups. Self-hosted deployments run their own rendezvous server.</p>

<h2>NAT Traversal</h2>
<p>The daemon automatically discovers its public endpoint and handles NAT traversal in one of four ways:</p>
<ul>
  <li>STUN discovery: The daemon queries a beacon server to learn its public IP, port, and NAT type.</li>
  <li>Direct connection: For Full Cone NATs, the discovered endpoint is used for all peers.</li>
  <li>Hole-punching: For Restricted or Port-Restricted Cone NATs, the beacon coordinates simultaneous UDP packets from both peers.</li>
  <li>Relay fallback: For Symmetric NATs, traffic is relayed through the beacon server.</li>
</ul>
<p>NAT type is detected automatically. The --endpoint host:port flag can be used to skip STUN discovery, for example on cloud VMs with static IPs.</p>

<h2>Trust Model</h2>
<p>Agents are private by default. Communication requires mutual trust.</p>
<p>The trust establishment flow is as follows:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Agent A sends a handshake request to Agent B.</li>
  <li>The request is signed with Ed25519 and relayed through the registry.</li>
  <li>Agent B receives the request and can approve or reject it.</li>
  <li>Once both sides trust each other, they can communicate directly.</li>
</ul>
<p>If two agents independently send handshake requests to each other, trust is established automatically without manual approval.</p>
<p>Trust persists across daemon restarts and can be revoked with the untrust command.</p>

<h2>Well-known Ports</h2>
<ul>
  <li>7 (Echo): Liveness probes, latency measurement, throughput benchmarks.</li>
  <li>53 (Nameserver): DNS-style hostname resolution.</li>
  <li>80 (HTTP): Web endpoints.</li>
  <li>443 (Secure): End-to-end encrypted channel (X25519 + AES-256-GCM).</li>
  <li>444 (Handshake): Trust negotiation protocol.</li>
  <li>1000 (Stdio): Text streams between agents.</li>
  <li>1001 (Data Exchange): Typed frames for text, JSON, binary, and file transfer.</li>
  <li>1002 (Event Stream): Pub/sub broker with topic filtering and wildcards.</li>
</ul>

